[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Building Resilient REST APIs: Circuit Breakers, Bulkheads, and Retries"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In modern distributed systems, network partitions, server crashes, and downstream service failures are inevitable. When building REST APIs that rely on other microservices or external databases, a single point of failure can cascade through your entire architecture. To ensure high availability, developers must implement robust resiliency patterns such as Retries with Exponential Backoff, Circuit Breakers, and Bulkheads."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Retries with Exponential Backoff"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Transient failures, such as momentary network glitches or brief database timeouts, often resolve themselves within seconds. A naive retry strategy will immediately hammer the struggling downstream service, worsening the outage. Exponential backoff introduces an increasing delay between retry attempts, combined with "jitter" to prevent synchronized retry storms."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"const axios = require("axios");
const wait = (ms) => new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, ms));

async function callWithRetry(url, retries = 3, delay = 1000) {
  try {
    return await axios.get(url);
  } catch (error) {
    if (retries === 0) throw error;
    console.log("Request failed, retrying in " + delay + "ms...");
    await wait(delay);
    return callWithRetry(url, retries - 1, delay * 2);
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Circuit Breakers"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"If a downstream service is completely down, retrying requests indefinitely wastes precious CPU cycles and thread pools. A Circuit Breaker monitors failures. When failures exceed a defined threshold, the circuit "trips" and opens. While open, any incoming requests fail fast immediately without hitting the broken dependency, giving the downstream service time to recover."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Closed State: Normal operation. Requests flow freely to the downstream service. Failures are counted."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Open State: The failure threshold is reached. Requests immediately throw an error without calling the external API."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Half-Open State: After a cool-down period, a limited number of test requests are allowed through. If they succeed, the circuit closes. If they fail, it opens again."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Bulkheads"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Named after the iron partitions in a ship's hull that prevent water from flooding the entire vessel, the bulkhead pattern isolates resources. In a REST API, you should restrict the number of concurrent connections or threads that can be allocated to a specific external service. If Service A slows down and exhausts its assigned thread pool, Service B remains entirely functional because its resources are safely compartmentalized."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Implementing these three patterns transforms fragile REST APIs into resilient, self-healing systems. Use Retries with Exponential Backoff for transient errors, Circuit Breakers to stop cascading failures, and Bulkheads to isolate system resources effectively."}]
